(node:10732) [CLAUDE_SDK_CAN_USE_TOOL_SHADOWED] Warning: canUseTool will not be invoked for: Read, Glob, Grep, WebFetch, WebSearch. Bare allowedTools entries auto-approve the whole tool before the callback is consulted. To gate every tool call, use a PreToolUse hook; or remove the bare names from allowedTools so they fall through to canUseTool. Allow rules from settings files can also shadow the callback but are not visible here.
{"t":"2026-10-22T18:05:20.815Z","event":"finish","launch":"0050-demo-builder","n":2,"role":"demo-builder","ok":true,"reason":null,"tokens":82607,"cumulative":782702,"cost":0.7323,"turns":11,"minutes":2.47,"sessions":["S-1"],"abandoned":[],"denials":0,"said":"I worked two items, both the draft stage of the eight-cores-not-eight-times lesson. Both passed the draft check and went on to the fact-checker's plan check. The session then hit its cap of 2 and closed.\n\n1. **First draft (R-1), passed.** I wrote `src/demos/cs/eight-cores-not-eight-times/meta.json` with status `draft` and six claims covering the brief's four planned claims. All six were `unverified`:\n   - c1: the 8-core result, 20.6 min and 2.91x.\n   - c2: the 2-core and 4-core values on the way.\n   - c3: unlimited cores, 15 min and at most 4x.\n   - c4: Amdahl's law, citing Amdahl 1967 and Hennessy & Patterson.\n   - c5: the recall answer's explanation.\n   - c6: the stage caption stating the model's assumptions.\n\n   The plan check sent it back. It verified c1–c5 and disputed c6: the caption's \"(real speed-ups are lower still)\" isn't true of real hardware in general, because speed-ups beyond the number of cores do happen.\n\n2. **Revised draft (R-6), passed.** I removed that parenthetical from c6 and set c6 back to `unverified` with no checkedBy/checkedOn. I left c1–c5 exactly as the fact-checker verified them. It is back at plan check (loop 1).\n\nFor the build stage: the 4-core time has to be shown as 26.25, because rounding to one decimal shows 26.3. I put this in the hand-in note.\n\nTwo things confused me:\n- **Claim 4's support type.** The brief lists it as \"definition\", which the checks don't accept. The allowed values are derivation, simulation, source and convention, so I used \"convention\".\n- **Two claims share a brief number.** I gave the recall explanation (c5) its own claim, numbered 3 along with c4, and numbered c2 the same as c1. Both point back to the brief's planned claims, as required, but the brief didn't plan those two sentences separately."}
{"t":"2026-10-22T18:05:54.477Z","event":"finish","launch":"0058-demo-builder","n":3,"role":"demo-builder","ok":true,"reason":null,"tokens":70465,"cumulative":646744,"cost":0.5807,"turns":10,"minutes":2.92,"sessions":["S-2"],"abandoned":[],"denials":0,"said":"I finished one item: the draft for LESSON-faster-link-same-wait (O-2). It passed code's draft check with no findings and has moved on to the fact-checker's plan check. Nothing else was waiting, so the session has ended.\n\n**What I wrote:** only `src/demos/cs/faster-link-same-wait/meta.json`, in my own worktree. Its status is `draft`, it has the brief fields, and it has 6 claims, all `unverified` and each tied to one of the brief's 3 planned claims:\n- **Planned claim 1 (the toy-model numbers):**\n  - c1: the 510 / 505 / 60 ms totals.\n  - c2: 110 ms when sending 5 per round. The brief's planned claim 1 leaves this number out, but the trace can show it.\n  - c3: \"waiting, not sending, was the cost\" and \"more than eight times faster\".\n  - c6: the sentence explaining the recall answer, which uses 2 ms to send and an 80 ms wait.\n- **Planned claim 2:** c4, that latency depends on distance and signal speed, so a faster link can't remove it.\n- **Planned claim 3:** c5, the sentence about TCP keeping many packets in flight, sourced to RFC 9293 section 3.8. I flagged the brief's rule for the fact-checker: if they can't confirm that section, the last reveal sentence gets cut at build.\n\nI re-ran all the arithmetic in node: 510, 505, 110 and 60 ms all hold.\n\n**Two things that may confuse:**\n- The concept `latency-vs-bandwidth` isn't in `content/curriculum/cs.json` yet. That file belongs to cm-cs, so I left it alone and noted it in my leave. The draft check didn't object.\n- The brief gives no recall question, so I wrote one myself. It and its explanation are mine, not the brief's, and c6 covers the explanation."}
