{"t":"2026-10-22T18:07:39.916Z","event":"finish","launch":"0087-fact-checker","n":5,"role":"fact-checker","ok":true,"reason":null,"tokens":62114,"cumulative":1086112,"cost":1.0396,"turns":28,"minutes":3.62,"sessions":["S-4"],"abandoned":[],"denials":0,"said":"I ran four plan checks and the session closed when it reached the cap of 4. Two lessons pass to build; one goes back to draft.\n\n| # | Lesson | Verdict | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | eight-cores-not-eight-times | revise → draft | c6 disputed. The caption said \"(real speed-ups are lower still)\" as a fact about real hardware. That isn't always true: work can run more than N times faster on N cores, for example when its data fits in their combined caches. The math only shows it inside the toy model. The other claims, c1–c5, check out: I re-ran the numbers in node and confirmed the Amdahl (1967) paper and its DOI in Crossref. |\n| 2 | faster-link-same-wait | revise → draft | c5 (the TCP sliding-window sentence, citing RFC 9293 §3.8) stays unverified. The proxy blocks every RFC host I tried, so I couldn't read the section. The brief says to cut that sentence if it can't be confirmed. I also noted that window management is probably §3.8.6, not §3.8, but I couldn't confirm that either. All the other claims check out. |\n| 3 | drivers-stop-switching | pass → build | All 7 claims verified. I checked c1–c4 with my own node simulation of all 64 starting arrangements, and confirmed the Rosenthal (1973) and Monderer–Shapley (1996) papers in Crossref. |\n| 4 | eight-cores-not-eight-times (redraft) | pass → build | The builder dropped the parenthetical and changed nothing else (I diffed against my first check). All 6 claims are now verified. |\n\nOne thing for the builder on eight-cores: claim c2 says the 4-core time is 26.25 minutes, but `toFixed(1)` would display \"26.3\". The readout needs two decimals to match the claim.\n\nThings that confused me:\n- The first three items each had their own worktree (four items, three worktrees), and the shell kept resetting to the first worktree. I used absolute paths throughout.\n- In drivers-stop-switching, claim c6 has no `brief` field. It's a set-up rule, not one of the five planned claims, and code's draft check accepted it, so I logged it as an observation only.\n- For eight-cores, `meta.version` stayed at 1 across the redraft. That may be expected at the draft stage.\n- Crossref works through the proxy and is a good fallback for confirming journal citations when the publisher's own site is blocked."}
